 i# Client-Server and Web Communication

## Introduction
- Earlier computers worked independently (no communication).
- Networks enabled data sharing and communication.
- Early networks: ARPANET, CYCLADES, NPL.
- Modern networks evolved from these systems
- Systems now provide services to each other
  
## Key Concepts
- Client: Initiates request.  
- Server: Provides requested service. 
- Network: Medium for communication.
- Protocol: Rules for communication. 
- Port: Identifies specific service
- DNS: Resolves domain names to IP

## Client and Server
- Client initiates request.
- Server processes and responds
- Example:
  - Browser = Client
  - Website host = Server
- Client always starts communications.

## Request and Response
- Client sends request to server.
- Server sends response back.
- If error → server returns error message.
- Example:
  - Request → Ask for webpage
  - Response → Webpage or error 

## Protocol
- Defines rules for communication.
- Includes:
  - Commands (e.g., GET)
  - Structure of request
  - Syntax / language
  - Response types
  - Error handling

## Port
- Identifies a specific service on a server.
- Required to access correct service.
- One server → multiple services → multiple ports.
- Example: Different doors = different ports.

## DNS (Domain Name System)
- Converts domain name → IP address.
- Works like GPS.
- IP address = actual location of server.

## HTTP (HyperText Transfer Protocol)
- Used for web communication.
- Stateless → no memory of previous requests.
- Each request handled independently.
- Sessions (cookies/tokens) used for login persistence.

## HTTP Methods
- GET → Retrieve data
- POST → Send data
- PUT → Update data
- DELETE → Remove data
- PATCH → Partial update
- HEAD → Headers only
- OPTIONS → Available methods
- CONNECT / TRACE → Debugging

## GET Method
- Used to retrieve data from server.
- Example: GET /index.html
- Browser automatically sends GET requests.

## HTTP Request Structure
- Scheme → HTTP / HTTPS
- Host → Server name
- Path → Requested file
- Address → IP address
- Status → Success / Failure (e.g., 200 OK)

## HTTP Response Structure
- Two parts:
  - Response Header → Metadata
  - Response Body → Actual content (HTML, JSON, etc.)

## Summary
- Client sends request, server responds.
- Communication follows protocols (HTTP).
- DNS resolves domain to IP.
- Ports identify services.
- HTTP is stateless but sessions maintain continuity.
